<worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="activity-1-1-4-worksheet">
    <title>1.1.4 Example 2: Computing AB</title>

    <page>

        <p><m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are the matrices</p>

        <me>
            A =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A_{11} \amp A_{12}
            \end{pmatrix}
            \qquad
            B =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                B_{11} \\
                B_{21}
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>where</p>

        <me>
            A_{11} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix},
            \quad
            A_{12} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \\
                1
            \end{pmatrix},
            \quad
            B_{11} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1
            \end{pmatrix},
            \quad
            B_{21} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>If we compute the matrix product using the given partitioning we obtain</p>

        <me>
            AB =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A_{11} \amp A_{12}
            \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
                B_{11} \\
                B_{21}
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A_{11}B_{11} + A_{12}B_{21}
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>where</p>

        <me>
            A_{11}B_{11} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1
            \end{pmatrix}
            \qquad
            A_{12}B_{21} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \\
                1
            \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                0 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>Therefore</p>

        <me>
            AB =
            A_{11}B_{11} + A_{12}B_{21} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1
            \end{pmatrix} +
            \begin{pmatrix}
                0 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>Computing <m>AB</m> with the row column method confirms our result. </p>

        <me>
            AB =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 + 0 + 0 \amp -1 + 0 + 1 \\
                0 + 0 + 0 \amp 0 - 1 + 1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>
    </page>
</worksheet>